{
  "abstract": "We classify common compatible entries over an elliptic function field for a rational Diophantine family containing the zero pair $\\{3/4,-4/3\\}$. Let $E:Y^2=(X+46)(X-35)(X-10)$, $T=(35,0)$, and $C(Q)=3(x(Q)-179)/(4(x(Q)+46))$, with $C(O)=3/4$. For generic $R\\in E$, the common entries over $\\mathbb Q(E)$ of $\\{3/4,-4/3,224/507,C(2R+T)\\}$ are exactly $3/4,-4/3,0,C(2R)$. Thus the generic quadruple has a unique distinct nonzero fifth entry, and its matched quintuple has no sixth section over this field. The proof combines complete rational-point calculations at three fibers with elliptic rigidity. Split two-descent lifts every section to an elliptic self-map; two complete fibers leave only fourteen candidate functions, and a rank-zero fiber plus exact nonsquare specializations finish the classification. The two positive-rank fiber calculations use bielliptic quadratic Chabauty, exhaustive local-height partitions, component coupling, analytic tail bounds, and finite-group sieves. We also determine an exact three-class image modulo four for the identity part of a further genus-two fiber, whose Jacobian has certified rank interval one to two. Its first Cassels–Tate pairing vanishes, explaining why that rank-reduction strategy cannot finish the point classification. Source-only certificates and independent finite arithmetic checks support the results. The classification persists after every squarefree-degree genus-one base change. Isolated rational extensions, higher-genus multisections, and the unrestricted septuple problem remain unresolved.",
